WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.440
What makes Mars Attacks! more relatable today&nbsp;
than ever before? Which movies beat the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.560
at the box office? How close did it come to&nbsp;
winning an Oscar? Stay tuned to find out!

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.880
As it turns out, Mars Attacks! wan't an original&nbsp;
idea conjured up out of thin air by Tim Burton.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.560
They may not be as popular as the source material&nbsp;
for other Burton projects like Batman or Alice in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:27.520
Wonderland, but the trading cards that Mars&nbsp;
Attacks! is based on still have a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:34.160
pop culture legacy. They were first unleashed into&nbsp;
the world in 1962 and quickly gained notoriety for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:40.480
depicting aliens attacking humans in vivid,&nbsp;
violent detail. Decades before the likes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:46.240
Independence Day made it commonplace to destroy&nbsp;
sacrosanct locales in blockbuster flicks, the Mars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:51.200
Attacks! trading cards were blowing up the Golden&nbsp;
Gate Bridge and setting fire to the White House.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.840
Parents were outraged, but kids were&nbsp;
enamored. Though these cards weren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.080
originally around for a very long time,&nbsp;
their distinctive visuals and grotesque&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:05.120
humor left a lasting impact on the era's&nbsp;
youngsters. They even proved enduring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:10.000
enough to inspire a big-budget movie decades&nbsp;
after they first petrified the populace!

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.920
The original Mars Attacks trading cards weren't&nbsp;
the only ones terrifying children with horrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:21.760
imagery. The Dinosaurs Attack! cards of the&nbsp;
1980s were just as scary, as they depicted dinos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:27.520
attacking unfortunate humans and each other. These&nbsp;
intense illustrations sparked the imaginations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.920
kids everywhere, and they still inspire plenty&nbsp;
of nostalgia to this day. Considering that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.280
level of fondness, it's no surprise that a film&nbsp;
adaptation of these cards was once in development.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.520
There was also a Dinosaurs Attack!&nbsp;
comic series, and the first issue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.760
released in 1991, reported that a film&nbsp;
version of the property was in development.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.360
But Tim Burton ultimately put that project&nbsp;
aside in favor of adapting Mars Attacks! once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:57.040
Jurassic Park became a huge hit. A pop culture&nbsp;
juggernaut like that would've made a Dinosaurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:02.880
Attack! movie seem like a cheap cash-grab. So&nbsp;
with that project still as dead as a T. rex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.840
those yearning to see Tim Burton's take on&nbsp;
dinos will just have to keep on waiting.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:11.840
Many of Tim Burton's films are&nbsp;
explicitly linked to the movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.880
that inspired him to become a&nbsp;
filmmaker in the first place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:20.640
1994's Ed Wood, for example, offered him the&nbsp;
chance to pay homage to a famous, or infamous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:26.480
B-movie director. Mars Attacks! is no exception,&nbsp;
and it even served as another chance for Burton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:32.000
to tip his hat to the works of Ed Wood. As the&nbsp;
director explained to Arts Beat L.A. in 1996,

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.160
"I wanted to do something fun with&nbsp;
a bunch of Martians with big brains.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.960
Basically, make a modern version of Plan 9&nbsp;
From Outer Space or The War Of The Worlds.'"

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:45.680
Burton was also of course inspired by the&nbsp;
Mars Attacks! trading cards, particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.200
how they fused gross-out sci-fi elements with&nbsp;
titillating imagery. According to Burton,

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.440
"This was very much in the spirit of the sixties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.200
kind of sci-fi movies that I was&nbsp;
trying to re-create and I remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.360
always liking those movies where there&nbsp;
was some sort of weird alien girl."

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:05.920
Paying tribute to seminal works of oddball&nbsp;
entertainment has worked like gangbusters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.640
for Burton over the course of his career, and&nbsp;
Mars Attacks! is just one spectacular example.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.960
Typically, quirky sci-fi movies that&nbsp;
pay tribute to something like trading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:18.000
cards don't end up with particularly star-studded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.280
rosters. But the Mars Attacks! cast is&nbsp;
absolutely packed with A-list talent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:28.320
Some of the famous faces include Jack Nicholson,&nbsp;
Glenn Close, Michael J. Fox, Danny DeVito,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:34.240
and even a cameo appearance from singer Tom&nbsp;
Jones, and that barely even scratches the surface.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:37.600
Getting so many big names for Mars&nbsp;
Attacks! was more than just something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.000
done to appease the marketing department. It&nbsp;
was a conscious choice on Tim Burton's part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.400
in order to further challenge himself as a&nbsp;
filmmaker. As he explained to Arts Beat L.A.,

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:51.040
"The only time I had ventured into bringing&nbsp;
together several high-profile stars was for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:56.080
the Batman movies and here I wanted to repeat&nbsp;
this experience on an even bigger scale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:59.200
There are more than 20 lead roles in Mars Attacks!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.240
so it was quite a challenge for&nbsp;
me to put together this cast."

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:06.640
Luckily, this assortment of big-name performers&nbsp;
threw themselves into this uniquely goofy world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:10.720
with gusto, proving their ability to fit&nbsp;
right into Burton's imaginative vision.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.840
"Tom, Tom, can I have an&nbsp;
autograph? Anybody got a pen?"

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:22.320
Today, every movie from Avengers: Endgame to&nbsp;
Hello, My Name is Doris gets an internet-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:27.840
marketing campaign. But back in 1996 when the&nbsp;
Mars Attacks! team was tasked with building hype,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.480
internet marketing for movies&nbsp;
was still in its infancy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:34.880
It was so much of an event when films&nbsp;
got promotional websites back then that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.440
Entertainment Weekly ran articles examining&nbsp;
every instance within a calendar year.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.160
EW writer Bob Strauss gave the Mars Attacks!&nbsp;
website a B. He offered praise for how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.320
many behind-the-scenes details it offered,&nbsp;
including sketches from Tim Burton himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:53.040
However, he criticized it for an overall lack&nbsp;
of distinction. Strauss' breakdown of the site,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:58.320
which featured cast bios and links to relevant&nbsp;
sites, paints a bare-bones picture. It certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:02.240
seemed to have lacked the kind of audience&nbsp;
engagement elements that are more typical of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.400
today's virtual movie marketing. But these&nbsp;
were the early days of online promotion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:11.520
so it's fair to cut it a little slack. Movies like&nbsp;
Mars Attacks! deserve our respect for paving the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:16.640
road for modern marketing. Without them, we may&nbsp;
never have ended up with viral TikTok dances.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:22.160
It may sound like a fantasy when compared&nbsp;
to today's cinematic landscape, but there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:26.640
was a time when CGI wasn't absolutely&nbsp;
everywhere. As late as the mid-90s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:32.320
this technology was still limited to only the most&nbsp;
major blockbusters. But then slowly but surely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:36.880
those films began popularizing the idea&nbsp;
that digital wizardry could do just about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.880
anything anyone could imagine. That&nbsp;
included realizing weird-looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.600
alien invaders who keep coming up with&nbsp;
cartoonish ways of dispatching humanity.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:49.600
Tim Burton has tended to embrace old-school&nbsp;
methods of storytelling and visual effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:53.360
in many of his projects. But the unique&nbsp;
aesthetic of Mars Attacks! necessitated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:57.680
the use of modern technology. As the&nbsp;
director admitted to Arts Beat L.A.,

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:01.760
"I knew we had to create the aliens&nbsp;
with the help of CGI animation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:06.320
Every type of animation has a different vibe&nbsp;
and it's something you can really analyze.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:10.400
And there is something about the computer&nbsp;
medium that seemed to work with these characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:14.960
because they are all the same and they have&nbsp;
a certain jumping quality to their movement."

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.400
Computer-generated animation did indeed turn&nbsp;
out to be a good fit for the otherworldly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.440
characters of Mars Attacks!, allowing it to&nbsp;
serve as a shining example of the 90s CG boom.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:27.480
"Funny little critters, ain't they?"

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:32.560
Mars Attacks! received mixed marks from&nbsp;
critics upon its initial release, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:37.840
Academy Awards have a long-standing hesitancy&nbsp;
towards recognizing science fiction films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:41.520
When you consider these two facts together,&nbsp;
it's awfully apparent that this film had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:46.000
little hope of scoring some Oscars love.&nbsp;
But there was still the possibility that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:51.120
it could manage to snag a sliver of golden&nbsp;
glory for a Best Visual Effects nomination.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:56.400
This dream looked like it might become a reality&nbsp;
when in January 1997, Variety reported that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:01.440
Mars Attacks! was indeed one of seven movies&nbsp;
shortlisted for a nomination in that category,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.640
alongside the likes of Mission:&nbsp;
Impossible and The Nutty Professor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.280
At the time, shortlisted titles were&nbsp;
whittled down to three final nominees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:14.480
who would then compete on Oscar night. Considering&nbsp;
that Burton's Batman Returns had been nominated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:18.880
for Visual Effects a few years earlier,&nbsp;
these hopes weren't without precedent. So a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.840
golden trophy for Mars Attacks! really did seem&nbsp;
possible within this brief window of optimism.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:27.200
Sadly though, Mars Attacks! made&nbsp;
it no further in the Oscar race.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:32.960
When the official nominees for Best Visual&nbsp;
Effects at the 69th Academy Awards were announced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:38.080
Independence Day, Twister, and Dragonheart made it&nbsp;
in, while Mars Attacks! was nowhere to be found.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:39.440
"What is wrong with you people?"

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:45.440
It's hard to imagine a Tim Burton movie without&nbsp;
an accompanying score from composer Danny Elfman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:49.920
Over the decades, Elfman has translated Burton's&nbsp;
distinctive spirit into a wide assortment of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:55.360
music, ranging from the moodily epic cues of&nbsp;
Batman movies to the darkly whimsical tones of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:59.760
Charlie and the Chocolate Factory. For Mars&nbsp;
Attacks!, Elfman channeled mid-20th century&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:04.720
music to match the retro visual aesthetic of&nbsp;
the film and pay tribute to its source material.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:09.440
Even an unfinished clip from Mars Attacks!&nbsp;
proved to be so vividly imagined that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:13.280
got Elfman's creative juices flowing.&nbsp;
It was pretty much a piece of cake for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:19.440
this experienced composer to conjure up the&nbsp;
necessary music. As he told Collider in 2019,

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:24.160
"I saw the opening for Mars Attacks!, when&nbsp;
Tim showed me just a simple pre-vis version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:29.040
and I said, 'Hold the film!' I ran out in the&nbsp;
lobby of the theater, and I started making all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:33.680
of these notes to myself, for what became the&nbsp;
theme. Usually, it's much harder than that."

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:35.920
With this sudden burst of creativity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:40.480
one of the most unforgettable parts of Mars&nbsp;
Attacks! was firmly cemented: the music.

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:47.200
By the time Mars Attacks! hit theaters in December&nbsp;
1996, Tim Burton had developed a reputation for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:52.000
helming lucrative fare, thanks to the likes&nbsp;
of Beetlejuice, Batman, and Batman Returns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:56.800
Alas, Mars Attacks! ended up being a step&nbsp;
backwards in terms of box office prowess.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.600
Grossing $37.7 million domestically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.160
the film ultimately earned just half of&nbsp;
what Beetlejuice amassed eight years prior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:09.760
It also came in below what Batman and Batman&nbsp;
Returns earned in just their opening weekends.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:14.960
The zany comedic sensibilities of Mars Attacks!&nbsp;
ended up resonating a little bit more overseas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:19.840
where the quirky feature grossed enough to put&nbsp;
the worldwide box office total at $101 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:24.960
Still, the lackluster financial reception remains&nbsp;
notable, considering its pedigree in front of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:28.880
and behind the camera. Perhaps Mars&nbsp;
Attacks! was seen as too much of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:34.560
Independence Day clone by the general public.&nbsp;
Maybe the exposed brains were a little too creepy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:38.400
Or perhaps its trading card&nbsp;
origin was just too niche.

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:43.200
Here's one thing we do know for certain:&nbsp;
December 1996 saw the release of a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:48.080
of mega-hit movies in the forms of Jerry&nbsp;
Maguire and Scream. Clearly, more moviegoers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:52.480
rushed out to see those films instead of the&nbsp;
considerably more expensive Mars Attacks!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:56.720
Burton had already experienced box office&nbsp;
disappointment a couple years earlier with Ed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:02.000
Wood, but this was his first genuine attempted&nbsp;
blockbuster to come up noticeably short.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.000
"Why can't we all just get along?"

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:15.840
At the time of its release, critics praised&nbsp;
Mars Attacks! for its visual effects and strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:21.360
performances, but they took umbrage with its&nbsp;
emphasis on stylized dark gags over compelling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:27.120
characters. All of the anarchic mayhem on&nbsp;
display just wasn't to everyone's tastes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:30.800
These lukewarm notices made Mars&nbsp;
Attacks! a noticeable step down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:34.640
in terms of critical reception from&nbsp;
prior Burton efforts like Beetlejuice.

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:38.560
But in the decades since it first hit&nbsp;
theaters, Mars Attacks! has managed to endure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:43.200
quite nicely in the public consciousness.&nbsp;
Websites like Collider and Consequence have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:48.320
published lengthy essays extolling its virtues,&nbsp;
with the latter site praising it for how much it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:53.200
commits to old-school cinema homages and&nbsp;
gruesome violence. Steve Desch of Slate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:59.200
meanwhile, opined that Mars Attacks! has only&nbsp;
grown more relevant since its 1996 debut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.200 --> 00:11:04.560
thanks to its depiction of politicians as being&nbsp;
utterly inept in the face of global catastrophe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:08.480
Desch also praised the film for committing&nbsp;
to the idea that a war between otherworldly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:13.440
invaders and human beings would be an incredibly&nbsp;
one-sided affair in favor of the Martians.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:17.920
The franchise's legacy can also be seen in&nbsp;
the enduring popularity of the trading cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:22.560
with new sets arriving in 2021. Mars&nbsp;
Attacks! may not have been universally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:26.320
beloved upon its initial release, but&nbsp;
some of its most outlandish qualities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:30.480
have aged remarkably well. Today, it's&nbsp;
treasured by the fans who understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:35.040
what it's going for and recognize it as a&nbsp;
standout effort in Burton's filmography.

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:40.640
"So I guess, like, now we just have to&nbsp;
start over and start rebuilding everything."

